[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 5"""
 date="2026-10-12T13:50:13Z"
 content="""
The fixes proposed above are incomplete in that an attacker can still learn
if I have a given key in given location.

`git-annex sync` will get it from the autoenabled special remote,
and update location tracking to say it's in my clone of the repository,
and pushes the git-annex branch to origin, exposing that information
to the attacker.

To prevent this, two options:

1. Have a pre-push hook that prevents all pushes when there is an
   autoenabled special remote that has not been verified.
2. Avoid committing the journal to the git-annex branch before an
   autoenabled special remote is verified.

The pre-push hook would need to be installed before autoenabling a special
remote. (If there's an existing pre-push hook that the user has installed
that doesn't call git-annex, the autoenabling would need to fail. This
would be an uncommon special case, since usually autoenabling happens at
first git-annex init, when the repository is young. Also use of pre-push
hooks is probably rare.)

Avoiding commiting the journal to the git-annex branch would be easier to
implement, but leaves the user with no indication why the git-annex branch
is not getting sent to remotes as usual. The pre-push hook can display a
useful message.
"""]]
